<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Age of Discovery</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Age of Discovery refers to the period from the fifteenth to the seventeenth century when European navigators, led by Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, explored oceans and established sea routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It shifted global trade towards Atlantic and oceanic routes. Its effects are discussed in relation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, whose overland trade routes were bypassed, and in comparisons with earlier Chinese maritime activity under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery.jpg" alt="Age of Discovery" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasco_Da_Gama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Age of Discovery is dated c. 1418 to c. 1620. Its background lay in trade. For centuries the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and neighbouring maritime republics held the monopoly of European trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and spice trade made them very rich. Muslim traders dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> routes, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> a main emporium, and the overland routes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> were blocked by wars and banditry as the Mongol states disintegrated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, though not a military success, had brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> into contact with the Middle East and its goods.</p><p>The Portuguese began systematically exploring the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1418 under the sponsorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, and in 1488 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> by that route. Portugal had rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s plan to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by sailing west in 1485 and 1488, its experts judging his estimate of the distance too low.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In 1492 the Catholic Monarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> funded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s Atlantic crossing, and he reached a continent unmapped by Europeans, though the Norse had explored it 500 years earlier. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>, ratified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, divided the zones of exploration between Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1498 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> established a sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Vasco Núñez de Balboa became the first European to see the Pacific from the New World on 25 September 1513, and the Magellan expedition of 1519–1522 under Juan Sebastián Elcano completed the first circumnavigation.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s conquistadors, who were more like soldiers of fortune than an army, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> (1519–1521) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> (1532–1533) and formed alliances with some indigenous peoples. In 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-urdaneta">Andrés de Urdaneta</a> found the return route across the Pacific, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> on 8 October 1565 after 130 days, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> trade linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia until 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> did not recognise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> and challenged Iberian power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, meanwhile, moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Contact between the Old and New Worlds produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a> of plants, animals, people, including slaves, diseases and culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a>, horses and sheep went west, and tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> came east. The era began the global <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It also brought the enslavement, exploitation and conquest of Indigenous peoples, and European diseases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> decimated indigenous populations.</p><p>The term itself has been scrutinised. J. H. Parry called the period the Age of Reconnaissance and stressed its expansion of geographical knowledge and empirical science, while Edmundo O'Gorman, as Anthony Pagden reports, argued that Europeans' effort to fit the new knowledge into their worldview mattered more than the encounter, in what he called the invention of America.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Age of Discovery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ottoman control of the eastern Mediterranean and its trade routes is often cited as one of the spurs to the <b>Age of Discovery</b>, the Portuguese and Spanish search for oceanic passages to Asia, so that the consequences of Mehmed II’s conquest reached far beyond the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The magnetic compass, described by Shen Kuo in 1088 and recorded in use for maritime navigation by the early twelfth century, later reached Islamic and European navigators and eventually contributed to the European voyages of the <b>Age of Discovery</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
